question_text:
3 cards are chosen at random from a standard 52-card deck. What is the probability that they form a pair? (A 3-card hand is a 'pair' if two of the cards match in rank but the third card is different. For example, 668 is a pair, but 999 is not.)
A) \frac{1}{8}
B) \frac{1}{36}
C) \frac{1}{78}
D) \frac{72}{425}

As punishment for bad behavior during recess, Mrs. Busywork asked her class to multiply 10 by $\frac{1}{3}$ five times. John, however, notices that it is possible to multiply 10 by a single fraction and still get the same answer as the other students. What is this single fraction?
A) \frac{1}{27}
B) \frac{1}{68}
C) \frac{1}{3}
D) \frac{1}{243}

Evaluate $\log_8 2$.
A) \frac{1}{4}
B) 4
C) 3
D) \frac{1}{3}

Suppose that we roll two fair 6-sided dice. What is the probability that the two numbers rolled sum to 4?
A) \frac{1}{4}
B) \frac{1}{12}
C) \frac{1}{3}
D) \frac{1}{9}
